WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:05.820
NATHAN MYHRVOLD: Cooking
is about using heat

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.000
to create chemical
reactions in the food.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.460
Cooked food isn't just hot food.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.930
It's fundamentally different.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.040
If you cook something and you put
it in the fridge and make it cold,

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.279
it's still cooked, it's just cold now.

00:00:19.279 --> 00:00:20.820
It doesn't go back to its same state.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.120
Whereas if you take a piece of
metal, make it hot, cool it down,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.070
it's pretty much the
same as it was before.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.940
There's a chemical, an
irreversible chemical change.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.540
Now chemistry teaches us
that chemical changes usually

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.780
require both time and temperature.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.290
And the rate at which a
chemical reaction will occur

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.810
is some function of
time and temperature.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:47.790
Broadly speaking, there's a whole set of
foods for which the chemical reactions

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:53.250
we call cooking happen very fast,
so fast that pretty much the instant

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.180
you reach a temperature, it's done.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.680
Now if it's not at that
temperature, it may not be done yet,

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.250
but doneness means bring to a
temperature and then you're done,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.870
that's it.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.620
There's another set of foods where the
chemical reactions are fundamentally

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.200
much more slow.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.690
And in those slow reactions,
we have to hold the food

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.570
at a particular temperature
for a period of time.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:24.180
Most foods that are in the fast group
would include fruits, vegetables, eggs,

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.990
and fish, and tender meats.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.140
If you are cooking a
filet mignon, as soon

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.950
as it reaches a certain
temperature, it's done.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.730
For me I like rare about
120 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:42.120
I like medium rare to be
about 130 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.410
And so the minute, the second, the
actually tiny fraction of a second it

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.960
reaches 130, it's done.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.520
But tough meats are
fundamentally different.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.500
Tough meats, that would
be cuts like brisket,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:03.360
like short ribs, like skirt steak,
any of these tough cuts of beef,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.400
or of any other meat, for that
matter, even tough stewing chickens

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:15.990
or the leg meat of ground-dwelling
birds like ducks or pheasants,

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:21.010
that tough meat is tough because it
has something called collagen in it.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.070
Now collagen is a topic
totally unto itself.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.030
But for our purposes
here, the thing that's

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.800
different about cooking
these tough meats

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.350
is that we have to cook them long
enough to soften that collagen.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.750
In traditional cooking, that usually
means cooking a stew or a braise.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:43.140
And a typical stew or braise you might
cook for an hour, two hours, three

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.590
hours, even overnight in some cases.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.170
And that's what traditional
cooking was about.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.440
It was about saying let's
cook this stuff very slowly,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.020
but at moderately high temperature.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:00.720
As a result, braised or stewed meat
tends to be gray, it tends to be dry.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.620
Because that high temperature,
also because of collagen,

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.360
winds up wringing the meat
out from the inside out.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.720
With sous vide cooking, we can cook
at a precise temperature for as long

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.610
as we like.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:19.140
And we can actually cheat
this collagen curve.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:25.080
We can get a rare or medium rare-like
meat that's still nice and red,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.040
it's still nice and juicy, but
still degrade the collagen and make

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.480
that meat tender.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.160
But to do that, we have to extend
our cooking times very long.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.520
That's because most
chemical reactions follow

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.150
what's called Arrhenius' law, or
Arrhenius' first law of chemical

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.720
reactions, that they're
exponential in temperature.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.610
So that means that the more
you increase the temperature,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:52.590
the faster they go at a dramatic rate.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:59.520
With most food chemical reactions, if
you drop the temperature by 10 degrees,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.730
you increase the cooking
time by a factor of 10

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.930
to get the same results if it's
in this slow set of cooking around

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:13.650
collagen. As a result, we will do
sous vide cooking for 24, 48, 72,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:18.160
or even in one example, an oxtail
recipe we have in modernist cuisine,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.489
100 hours.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:22.620
Now that requires a
little planning ahead.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.460
You don't come home from work and say,
hey hon, let's have 100-hour oxtail.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.670
You kind of have to plan it in advance.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.670
The interesting thing is by
cooking that oxtail for 100 hours,

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:38.700
or a short rib for 72 hours,
or pork ribs for 48 hours,

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.740
you get a texture that is totally
unlike what you would get from braising.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:47.340
It's delicious, it's unexpected.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:53.670
It's a way to take a meat and
reinvent it as something entirely new,

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.880
and use something that's both
got a lot of flavor in it

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.330
and is usually pretty cheap
because we generally price meats

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.870
according to how tender they are.

